"Since China is becoming more active in artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double the amount it expends on research in the field, says Kai-Fu Lee, the investor and user of artificial intelligence, who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments come after several U.S. government members have made announcements of the IA, albeit in the U.S., an official strategy for artificial intelligence is lacking.
In the meantime, China unveiled its plan last-development last year: it aims to come to the forefront in terms of IA's innovation by 2030.
"Doubling the budget for AI research would be a good start, given that all the other countries are much further back to the U.S. and that we're looking forward to the U.S."
Doubling the funds could doubling the chances that the next big Achievement will be made in the U.S., Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book "Super-Perpetrators AI: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order" was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is CEO of Synovation Ventures, who has invested in one of China's most prominent companies, Face ++.
In the 1980's at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an IA system that defeated the U.S. player Othello, who was the best in the field at that time, and later was chief executive of Microsoft Research and the president of the Google branch in China.
Lee praised previous competitions of U.S. tech governments, such as the Robotics Challenge of Advanced Defense Research Agency, and questioned when it would come, to help identify the next visionaries.
Scholedicators in the U.S. often have to work hard to obtain government funds, Lee said.
"It's not that China's buzzing the academic leaders, it's corporations," Lee said.
Facebook, Google, and other technology companies have hired outstanding graduates from universities to work at IA in recent years.
Lee said changes to the immigration policy could help the U.S. step up its IA efforts.
"I think it should automatically be passed on to PhD students in IA," he said.
China's State Council issued a New Artificial Intelligence Development Plan in July 2017.
The National Foundation for Natural Sciences of China provides financial support to individuals in academic institutions similar to how the National Foundation for Science and other governmental organizations provides money for researchers in the United States, but the quality of academic activity is lower in China, Lee said.
At the beginning of the year, the U.S. Department of Defense set up a Common Center on Artificial Intelligence for the Purpose of Involving Business Partners and Academics, and the White House announced the formation of an Artificial Intelligence Selection Commission.
This month the U.S. Army Research Institute (DARPA) announced an investment worth $2 billion for an “AI Next” initiative.
