Macedons to Vote in Referendendum If They Want Country's Name Change
On Sunday, the Macedonian citizens voted in a referendum if they wanted the country's name to be changed to the "Northern Republic of Macedonia", a change that would settle a one-degree-develop a decade-old dispute with Greece, which opposed its accession to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which encompasses a province dubbed Macedonia, argues that the country's neighbouring country's name represents a claim on its territory and has resisted its adherence to the EU and NATO.
The two-governments agreed in June over the proposed new name, but the opposition nationalists argue that this change would undermining the ethnic identities of Macedonia's slave-majority population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has stated that he will not vote in the referendum and that a boycott campaign has seeded a doubt regarding the voter turnaround, being likely not to reach the minimum limit of 50 per cent, necessary to validate the referendum.
“Do you agree to join EU and NATO membership, accepting the deal with Greece?”.
Supcorers, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say it is a price to be paid by Macedonia, one of the countries that has resulted in the break-up of Yugoslavia, in order to be accepted into entities such as the EU and NATO.
"I am here today to vote for the country's future, for young people in Macedonia to live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because this means safer lives for all of us," 7-Year-Year-Year-Year-Year-Year-Old Olivera Georgijvska said.
Although it is not legally mandatory, quite a number of members of parliament have said they will respect the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
In order in order for the name to be changed, a two-three quorum in parliament is required.
The state's election commission said no irregularities had been reported until 13:00 a.m.
However, the turnout was only 16%, compared with 34% in the last parliamentary elections of 2016 when 66% of the people with the right to vote came to vote.
"We've come to the vote for my children and for our place in Europe," Gjose Tanevski, 62-Year-O-Year-Yehalf-Old Voter from Skopje, the country's capital.
On September 30, 2018, Macedone's Prime Minister Zoran Zaeev, his wife, Zorica, as well as their son Dushko, voted for the referendum on changing Macedonia's name, which will allow them to join NATO and enter the European Union.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, the 54-year-a-Year-Year-Old, prepared a small-smatch scene and fired chairs in front of the tents raised by those who would boycott the referendum.
"We support NATO and the EU, but we would like us to join them with their heads held high, and not in the back door," Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we have dignity.
"If we're unwitting as Macedonia, we can turn to other countries, like China and Russia, and become part of the Eurasian Union."
Prime Minister Zayev has stated that joining NATO will bring much-necessary investments in Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20 per cent.
"I think the majority will be in favor of changing the name, because more than 80 per cent of our citizens are in favor of joining NATO and the EU," Zaev said after casting his vote.
He said that an outcome in favor of changing the name would "confirm our future."
According according to a poll published last month by the Policy Research Institute, between 30% and 43% of citizens with the right to vote will participate in the referendum, below the necessary number of participants.
According to an other poll conducted by Telma TV in Macedonia, 57 per cent of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 per cent said they would vote in favour of the change.
In order for the Referendum to be valid, voter turnout must be 50 per cent plus one ballot.
Failing of the referendum would represent the first serious blow to pro-Western government's queuing policy since taking over the government in May last year.
