The Labour Party no longer tolerates people with a different point of view.
People who express their opinion are no longer tolerated
I did not miss at all the fact that Momentum activists from my local party supported the no-confidence motion as far as I am concerned.
After all, I'm just one of the Members of Parliament's Laburitory Party who has been told they're not welcome, just because we've expressed our opinion.
Joan Ryan, my colleague in Parliament, received similar treatment as it was vehement about anti-Semitism.
As far as I'm concerned, the no-confidence motion was a critique against me for the fact that I didn't agree with what Jeremy Corbyn said.
Regarding the Importance of a Responsidible Economic Politsue, National Security (National Security), similar issues, irhronically, in respect of which Jeremy did not have common views with previous leaders.
Communication for the Labour Party's Nottingham East meeting noted that "we want our meetings to be inclusive and proficiency."
Largely from the eight-year period as a Member of Parliament for the Laburist Party, the GC meetings on Friday night were like this.
“Unfortunately, today, many meetings do not take place in this way, and the promise of a "better, milder" political environment has long been forgotten, if it has ever been respected.
It's become increasingly evident that different opinions are not tolerated in the Labour Party and that every opinion is judged according to what is accepted by the party's leadership.
Things started to be like this since Jeremy became a leader, when I noticed that some colleagues with whom I had a common vision in terms of politics started to expect to change my way of thinking and agree to some of the things that I previously would not have agreed, among these being the national security or the single market in the EU.
Every time I speak in the public, regardless of what I'm just saying, there's a wave of abuses on the social networks calling for my elimination from the party, denouncing centrist politics, telling me I shouldn't be in the Labour Party.
And I'm not the only person who's dealing with that.
Indeed, I know I'm luckier than my classmates because comments relating to my person tend to be of a political nature.
I am impressed by the professionalism and the determination of my peers who are confronted with a sexual or racist abuse, but maintaining their level of involvement in this regard.
One of the most despicable aspects of this political era is the fact that abuse has become a normal thing.
Jeremy Corbyn claimed last week that the Laburist Party was supposed to entourage a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we no longer accept different points of view within the party, and with each no-confidence motion or a change of selection rules, the party is becoming less and less flexible about this.
A lot of people have recommended in the last two years to put my head down, to stop being so vocal, and everything “it’s going to be all right” like this.
But it wasn't why I got into politics.
Since I joined the Lavish Party, 32 years ago, when I was a student, reasoned by the fact that the negligence of the Thatcher government led to the collapse of my class, I wanted to fight for improved public services for those who most in need of them, as a local councilor or minister.
I've never concealed my political views, including in the last election.
There's no person in Nottingham East being able to say that my views on politics and the issues I disagree with in relation to the current management.
The people who supported Friday's motion, I can only tell them that as the country moves towards Brexit, something that will affect our local households, businesses and services, I don't understand their willingness to waste time and energise my allegiance to the leader of the Labour Party.
However the only message I want to convey is not for the Momentum group in Nottingham, but for the Citizens, regardless of whether or not they are members of the Labour Party: I am proud to represent you and I promise you that no threat of removal from the party or political opportunism will stop me from taking the decisions I consider in your superior interest.
Chris Leslie is a Member of the Laburitory Party for Nottingham East
