John Edward: Linguistic Skills Essific for Global Citizens
Outgoing schools in Scotland maintain a record of academic excellence, and this continued in 2018 with another set of exceptional exam results, which is supported by individual and collective successes in sports, art, music and other community efforts.
With more than 30,000 students from Scotia, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), are striving to provide services at the highest level to pupils and their parents.
Self-stunate schools aim to prepare their pupils for high school and higher education, their chosen careers and their place as global citizens.
As an educational sector that can conceive and implement a special curricula, we note that modern languages continue to be a popular and desired topic of choice within schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak to a man in a language he understands, your words will come to his mind.
"If you speak to him in his own language, the words come to his heart."
It's a strong reminder that we can't just rely on English when we want to build relationships and trust people from other countries.
Based on the recent results of this year's competition, we note that foreign languages are at the top of the rankings, with the highest promotion rates within independent schools.
A total of 68% of pupils who have studied foreign languages have obtained a Higher Grade A certificate.
Data collected from 74 SCIS member schools demonstrated that 72% of the students obtained a Higher Grade A certificate in Mandarin; also 72% of those who study German, 69% of those who study French and 63% of those who study Spanish obtained such a certificate.
"This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as the vital skills that children and young people will certainly need in the future.
Now, foreign languages, as a subject, are held just like STEM (Knowledge, technology, engineering and mathematical subjects) in stand-alkay school curricula and in other fields.
"A survey conducted by the UK's Committee on Employment and Skills in 2014 found that for the reasons employers gave for the fight to fill vacancies, 17 per 7 per 7 per cent were attributed to a language proficiency shortage.
Therefore, more and more language skills are becoming imperative for the preparation of young people for their future career.
With more job opportunities that require foreign-language, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
No matter what career anyone chooses, if they have learned a second language, they will have a real advantage in the future, having such a degree throughout their lives.
Achievement to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically put a multilingual person ahead of the competition.
According to a survey by YouGov on more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and French was the only language spoken by two-digit, 15 per 15 per cent.
That's why investing in language learning is important for today's children.
Having several languages, especially in countries with the developing economy, children will have a better chance of finding significant jobs.
In Scotia, every school will teach other foreign languages.
A number of schools will focus on more-than-modern classical languages, while others will learn the languages that are considered to be the most important thing for the United Kingdom when looking ahead by 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child's interest, there'll always be more languages of your choice in self-sponsor schools with teachers who specialize in this area.
Scot's independent schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children and foster them with the skills they need to succeed, whatever the future.
It cannot be denied at this moment, in a global business environment, that languages continue to be vital to the future of the country, so it must be reflected in education.
Indeverevere, modern languages should indeed be considered "inter-international communication cognitions".
The independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellency for young people in Scotland.
I'm making it bien le faire (It has to be done).
