HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% increase in number of new cases
China has announced a 14 per cent increase in the number of Chinese citizens suffering from HIV and AIDS.
More than 820,000 people are affected by this disease in China, officials say.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported only in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases have been sexually transmitted, as opposed to those in the past.
In the past, HIV has spread rapidly in some areas of China as a result of transfusions with infected blood.
However, the number of people who have contracted HIV in this way has been reduced almost to zeros, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
From one year to the next, however, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100,000.
Sexual HIV transmission is an accrual problem in the LGBT community in China.
“Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discriminated against LGBT people is considered very prevalent.
Because of the country's conservative values, studies estimate that 70-90% of men who have sex with other men will eventually end up marrying a woman.
Many cases of disease-transmitting come from inadequate sexual protection measures in such relationships.
Since 2003, China's government has vowed universal access to medicines against HIV as part of an effort to resolvete the problem.
