“What is the Global Citizen’s Festival and it has managed to do something to reduce poverty?
On this Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizen's Festival, an annual Musical Event attended by an impressive group of stars with an equally impressive mission: the elimination of world poverty.
Arrived at its seventh edition, the Global Citizen's Festival will attract tens of thousands of people on Central Park, not only to watch artists like Janet Jackson, Cardi B or Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the event's true goal, the elimination of extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen's Festival, launched in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to eliminate poverty by increasing the number of people who are actively striving against it.
In order to receive a free ticket to the event (unfortunesably if they are willing to pay for a VIP ticket), viewers have had to perform a series of tasks or "actions", such as volunteering, sending e-mails to world leaders, making phone calls or other significant things that are helping to raise the goal of eliminating global poverty.
However, what success did the Global Citizen’s Festival make when there are only 12 years left to achieve the goal?
It's the idea of rewarding people with a free concert a good way to convince them to act or is it just another case of so-called "clicktivism", which refers to people who feel they have achieved something by signing an online petition or sending a message on Twitter?
According to Global Citizen, more than 19 million "shares" have been recorded since 2011 from its proponents, who promoted different goals.
The organization says that these actions have spurred world-leading leaders to announce commitments and policies worth more than $37bn, which will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In the beginning of 2018, the group was talking about 390 engagements and announcements caused by its actions, in whose account at least $10 billion have already been Spawned or Raised.
The group estimates that insured funds so far had a direct impact on almost 649 million people from all over the world.
