Inventurer World Wide Web Plans to Set Up a New Internet to Compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, launches a startup that wants to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The Lamult Technary Legend's Latest Project, Insrupt, is a company based on Solid, Berners-Lee's open-sourlage platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and who has the right to access the information.
In an out-of-exquisite interview with the Fast-County, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inrupt is "global dominion."
"Now we have to do it," he spoke about the startup.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses Solid technology to allow people to create their own "personal online data archives" or PODs.
It can contain lists of contacts, programs, calendars, music libraries or other personal and professional instruments.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify would all be available in one browser, all at once.
What is unique about this online personal data archive is that the user has complete control over who is able to access this type of information.
The company calls this "Personal Consolidation through Data."
According to the company's CEO, John Bruce, the idea is for Infuture to contribute appropriate resources, processes, and competencies in such a way as to make Solid available to everyone.
At present, the company is composed of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, a team of developers who work on the project and a community of volunteer programmers.
