Japan's automaker said the impact of border delays in the event of a no-deal Brexit could cost jobs.
The plant at Burnaston, where Toyota's Auris and Avensis brands are manufactured, produced nearly 150,000 cars last year, of which 90 per cent were exported to the rest of the European Union.
"My opinion is that if Britain leaves the EU at the end of March, production will stop at our factory," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's chief executive at Burnaston.
Other UK carmakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have said they are wary of exiting the EU without an agreement on cross-Balco-trade.
BMW, for example, says that, after Brexit, it will close the factory in Oxford where the Mini is produced for a month.
The main concerns are created by what producers call supply-associated risks in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production line works based on "fix-fix-to-e-time" principle: for custom-manufactured cars, parts arrive at 37-minute intervals from suppliers in the UK and the EU.
If the United Kingdom leaves the EU without an agreement on March 29, there could be problems at the border, which, according to industrial producers, could cause delays and a shortage of parts.
It'd be impossible for Toyota to store more inventory for a day at its Derbyshire plant, the company says, so production would be halted.
Clark said Theresa May's Chequers plan for forthcoming relations with the EU is "precisely calibrated to avoid these delays at the border."
"There must be an agreement. We would like to have the best deal, which will allow us not only to enjoy today's success, but also to seize this opportunity," he told BBC Radio 4.
"Evidence coming not only from Toyota but also from other manufacturers shows that we need to be able to continue to be able to use what has been a very successful supply chain set."
Toyota couldn't say how much time production would need to be halted, but cautioned that increased prices could reduce the plant's long-haison capacity and ultimately lead to job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working at Burnaston for 24 years and in charge of the U.N. trade union at the plant, said members are increasingly worried: "Experience has taught me that after they disappear, these jobs don't come back."
A government spokeswould say: "We've set up a precise and credible plan for the future relationship with the EU."
