North Korean fugitives drops below Kim's rule
The number of North Korean fugitives in South Korea has waned since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, a South-Korean lawmaker claimed.
Park Byeong-seug, citing South Korea's unification ministry, said about 1,127 people fled the country, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Park said that the key factors were more severe border controls between North Korea and China and higher fares levied by those illegally transporting people across the border.
Pyongyang has made no public comment.
A vast majority of fugitives from the north are eventually offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul officials say more than 30,000 North Koreans have illegally smuggled the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most are fleeing through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the heavily protected demilitarized zone (DMZ) between Korea's two parts.
